WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:04.520
When a movie is really great, we don't always
notice if a small part of the story doesn't

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:05.550
make much sense.

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:09.860
Sometimes, though, a few problematic details
can dampen all that movie magic.

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:13.480
Suspension of disbelief is one thing, but
huge, gaping plot holes?

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.460
These movies are something else entirely.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:17.300
The flyers in the vault

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.980
Ocean's 11 has so much going for it.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:24.669
A stellar cast, an action-packed story and
a triumphant ending where the crew makes off

00:00:24.669 --> 00:00:29.360
with $150 million dollars of casino money
after pulling off an incredible switcheroo.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:33.260
It all goes down when Danny Ocean and his
crew tell casino boss Benedict they'll blow

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:36.400
up his vault of cash, unless he puts it in
a waiting van.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.220
“May I ask how you expect to leave?

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:43.380
You believe I'm going to allow you to carry bags of
my money out of my casino doors?

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.500
No, you're gonna carry it out for us.”

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:50.000
Benedict does as he's told, but hedges his
bets by calling in a SWAT team and having

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:51.560
his flunkies follow the money.

00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:53.400
And when they finally intercept the vehicle?

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:58.370
The bags are full of x-rated flyers and the
"SWAT team" back at the casino is revealed

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:02.420
to actually be Ocean and his team in disguise,
making off with the real loot.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:03.280
Great, right?

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.960
That is, until you stop to wonder how the
fake "money" got in the vault in the first

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:07.460
place.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.280
The only people who've been in there are Yen,
who sneaks in via a tiny cart, and Ocean and

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.500
Caldwell, who rappel down an elevator shaft.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:15.880
None of them have bags.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Even director Steven Soderbergh just kind
of shrugs when asked about the plot hole in

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:21.250
the director's commentary.

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:23.060
The bottom line: No one knows, man.

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:23.940
Just enjoy the heist.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:25.420
Illegal kicks

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:31.000
The closing scenes of The Karate Kid see Mr.
Miyagi training Daniel to get him to the semi-finals

00:01:31.009 --> 00:01:35.070
of a local tournament, where one of the villainous
Cobra Kai competitors disables him with an

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:36.220
illegal kick to the knee.

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:39.820
Daniel powers through and goes on to meet
his nemesis Johnny in the finals.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:44.619
After Johnny does further damage to his injured
leg, Daniel assumes a secret "crane" stance

00:01:44.619 --> 00:01:49.179
and deals a whopping kick to Johnny's noggin,
winning both the match and the love of Johnny’s

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:49.780
ex-girlfriend.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:54.280
Except, of course, that the ref has repeatedly
stated that blows to the head are illegal.

00:01:54.289 --> 00:01:56.119
So who actually won that fight?

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:57.200
Probably just the audience.

00:01:57.940 --> 00:01:58.800
Telling the story

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:03.820
The Social Network chronicles the birth of
Facebook mostly in flashback, with Mark Zuckerberg

00:02:03.829 --> 00:02:08.069
and Eduardo Saverin giving their depositions
about the lying, cheating, and backstabbing

00:02:08.069 --> 00:02:11.970
that happened along the way to creating the
ubiquitous social networking platform.

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:15.890
This method of storytelling allows the movie
to jump between the past and present, offers

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:19.800
conflicting sides of the story, and generally
just keeps the action moving along.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:23.020
“I was your only friend.”

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:27.540
But there's a sticking point: In one scene,
Zuckerberg and Saverin are hooking up with

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:30.140
Facebook groupies in the bathroom of the club
they're at together.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:33.220
So many viewers have asked: who's giving the
deposition now?

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:37.190
It's pretty likely that this is just Zuckerberg's
memory instead of the deposition… so why

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:39.280
is he remembering the other guy's hookup?

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:40.120
Creep city.

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:42.080
No matter it's still entertaining.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:44.560
Hence the four Golden Globes and three Academy
Awards.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:45.740
Missing

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:50.040
The Hangover is one of the funniest, raunchiest,
wildest comedies ever.

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:54.020
The plot centers around Phil, Stu and Alan’s
attempts to track down their missing friend

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:56.280
Doug after a night of debauchery in Vegas.

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:58.440
After two days, the guys finally find Doug.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:02.760
It turns out that he’s actually on the roof
of the Mirage Hotel in which they're all staying,

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:04.680
sunburnt but otherwise no worse for wear.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:09.340
After the relief wears off, we're left to
wonder: how did he go unnoticed for that long?

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:13.580
Don't casinos have crazy security and cameras
everywhere including rooftops?

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:17.700
Besides, wouldn't Doug have tried to somehow
get noticed during those long 48 hours?

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:19.900
Seems like he could've at least yelled or
something.

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:21.040
Future tech

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:25.660
The main gist of Interstellar’s story is
that the world is being plagued by a mysterious

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:28.520
blight that destroys crops and depletes oxygen
levels.

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:32.960
The resulting food shortages and breathing
problems mean all of humanity is on the brink

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:33.760
of extinction.

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:38.760
So NASA puts together a team to head off on
an intergalactic journey to find new inhabitable

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:39.260
planets.

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:41.640
“We’re not meant to save the world.

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:42.960
We’re meant to leave it.”

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:47.280
Along the way, Coop, the pilot who accepts
the assignment to lead the mission, gets hurled

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:51.390
around the galaxy, at one point ending up
in a black hole where he can communicate with

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:55.540
his daughter Murph through a "tesseract" behind
her bookcase and doesn't get back home until

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:56.690
she's an old woman.

00:03:56.690 --> 00:03:57.180
Make sense?

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:58.180
Well, sort of.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:03:59.940
Time and space do crazy things.

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:01.570
But you know what else is problematic?

00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:05.530
The fact that even before Coop heads off to
the Gargantua galaxy, it's revealed that the

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:09.600
technology to artificially grow crops on other
planets already exists.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.340
So why not just grow them on Earth?

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:15.660
It's such a big plot hole that the movie's
novelization actually address it: the Blight

00:04:15.671 --> 00:04:17.549
got into the bio-domes too.

00:04:17.549 --> 00:04:18.049
Whoops.

